title: lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/tw5/tiddlers/sigil-hoolele
tags: $:/tags/Global lar:///ha.ka.ba/tags/SharktoothSigil
lar-kind: concurrency
lar-pono-uri: lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/hoolele
lar-see-also: \spawn
lar-pattern: <<~\s*hoolele\s+to=`([^`]*)`\s*>>
lar-example: <<~ hoolele to=`lar:///a.b.c/x`>>
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

<!-- hoolele — a NEW TASK ROOT at a target, outside the tree that launched it. THE TARGET RIDES AS
     `to`, one NAMED, TICKED value:

         <<~ hoolele to=`lar:///a.b.c/x`>>

     //hoʻolele// causes to fly — the kite released, the canoe with no line back to shore. The task
     outlives the scope that created it; no scope exit reaches it, and only an explicit Cancel() on
     the handle it returns ends it. The one unstructured move in the family: it hides the suspends
     effect from its caller, which supplies the escape.

     PREFER `lele` FOR ANYTHING LOSABLE. hoolele answers the three cases whose work MUST land —
     persistence across scope destruction, cross-island work, a background daemon loop.

     A BARE ADDRESS REACHES NOTHING. `lar:///x` standing bare binds a parameter named `lar` and the
     slot receives nothing; `to=` and one pair of ticks fill it.

     THE PAGE STAYS CLEAR. A launch governs execution and never layout, so the definition hands the
     compile layer a data annotation carrying the target and renders nothing. -->

\procedure ~hoolele(to:"")
<span class="lar-hoolele" data-hoolele-target=<<to>> data-cancellation="explicit" hidden="true"/>
\end
